    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n26172" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lēgĭfer</span></b> ,
			fĕra, fĕrum, adj.
		lex-fero, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>lawgiving</i> (<b>poet.</b>).
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Adj.</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Minos,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					Am. 3, 10, 41</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">legifera Ceres (as the foundress of the social
					life of mankind),</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Verg.
					A. 4, 58</span></i>
			. —</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Subst.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lēgĭfer</span></b> ,
			ĕri, m., <i>a
				law-giver</i>, applied to Moses, <i><span class="en">Lact.
				4, 17, 7</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Tert. Apol. 19 <i>fin.</i></span></i>; Prud. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">στεφ</span>. 3, 363; <i><span class="en">Vulg. Isa.
				33, 22</span></i>.</div>
